---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CTA from '../components/CTA.astro';
---

<Layout>
  <section class="page-intro">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">TECHNOLOGY, WITH PERSPECTIVE</p>
      <div class="intro-split">
        <h1>
          Intelligence with
          <br />
          <em>a visible process.</em>
        </h1>
        <p class="intro-copy">
          A useful system should make its reasoning easier to inspect. CIVOLYN’s
          conceptual architecture begins with context, keeps decisions visible
          and leaves room for people to disagree.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="intro-rule">
        <span>
          A proposed product architecture, not production infrastructure.
        </span>
        <span>CONCEPTUAL SYSTEM / 02</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container" aria-label="Conceptual architecture">
    <h2 class="sr-only">Conceptual architecture</h2>
    <div class="architecture-diagram">
      <div class="arch-node">
        <small>01 / CONTEXT</small>
        <h3>Information sources</h3>
        <p>Approved briefs, team playbooks and relevant operational records.</p>
      </div>
      <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      <div class="arch-node primary">
        <small>02 / ORCHESTRATION</small>
        <h3>A visible working process</h3>
        <p>
          Scoped retrieval, suggested steps, readable state and a place to
          pause.
        </p>
      </div>
      <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      <div class="arch-node">
        <small>03 / PRESENTATION</small>
        <h3>Results with context</h3>
        <p>
          Source references, clear status and information people can question.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="architecture-review">
        <span aria-hidden="true">↕</span>
        <b>Human oversight</b>
        <br />
        Review · correct · approve · stop
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="section-caption">
      <span>From context, through orchestration, to presentation.</span>
      <span>Human oversight applies throughout.</span>
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">THE PRINCIPLES BEHIND THE IDEA</p>
          <h2>
            Capability matters.
            <br />
            <em>So do its boundaries.</em>
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          These are design intentions for an imagined platform. They are not
          claims about implemented security controls, model performance or
          certified infrastructure.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="principles-grid">
        <article>
          <span class="number">01 / SCOPED INFORMATION</span>
          <h3>The right context, for the task.</h3>
          <p>
            A future system would need explicit permissions for each information
            source. Access to one workspace should not imply access to
            everything a team knows.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="number">02 / TRACEABLE OUTPUT</span>
          <h3>A reference you can inspect.</h3>
          <p>
            An assisted summary should identify the material it draws from and
            make uncertainty visible. When sources conflict, the interface
            should help a reader see the disagreement.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="number">03 / EXPLICIT CONTROL</span>
          <h3>A person owns the decision.</h3>
          <p>
            Proposals and actions deserve different treatment. A draft may be
            useful without being approved. Consequential steps should have a
            clear owner, a review point and a way to stop.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="number">04 / LEGIBLE STATE</span>
          <h3>Know what has happened.</h3>
          <p>
            Waiting, suggested, approved and complete mean different things. The
            interface should preserve those distinctions rather than make
            activity look like certainty.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section dark-section">
    <div class="container split-section">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">AI AS AN ASSISTANT TO JUDGMENT</p>
        <h2>
          Make a suggestion useful.
          <br />
          <em>Make its limits visible.</em>
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          Language models can produce fluent text that is incomplete or wrong.
          Retrieval can supply irrelevant context. A confident interface can
          make both harder to notice.
        </p>
        <p>
          The CIVOLYN concept puts references, review and clear state close to
          the output. It imagines assistance with preparation and discovery,
          while leaving decisions with an informed person.
        </p>
        <a class="arrow-link" href="/journal/#human-judgment">
          Read the essay on human judgment <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container tech-two-col">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">WHAT THIS WEBSITE ACTUALLY RUNS</p>
        <h3>A small, static frontend.</h3>
        <p>
          Astro generates the pages as HTML. A little TypeScript handles
          navigation and local previews. Fonts, artwork and styling are served
          from the same site.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Flow updates a four-step example in browser memory.</li>
          <li>Knowledge searches prepared sample text on the page.</li>
          <li>Signal selects between three fixed example datasets.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">WHAT A REAL PRODUCT WOULD STILL NEED</p>
        <h3>The difficult work behind the screen.</h3>
        <p>
          A production platform would require a separately designed and
          evaluated backend, permission model, data lifecycle, audit trail and
          operational support.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Real identity and source-level authorisation.</li>
          <li>Model evaluation, error handling and safety controls.</li>
          <li>Verified infrastructure, privacy practices and reliability.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <CTA title="Explore the thinking through the experience." />
</Layout>
